      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Once the transverse wave reaches the thirteenth ball, it and the first ball vibrate identically.\nBoth pass equilibrium moving left together (Figure 70e)."
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "After another $\\frac{1}{4}$ of a period, both reach their maximum leftward displacement (Figure 70f).\nTheir phase is always the same."
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "**Wavelength is the distance between nearest in-phase points**.\nThe first and thirteenth, second and fourteenth, and third and fifteenth balls share this spacing (Figure 70e, f)."
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "blockIndex": 1,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "For the longitudinal wave in Figure 71b, a wavelength separates the second and fourteenth balls or the fourth and sixteenth.\nWavelength is denoted by the Greek letter $\\lambda$."
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "blockIndex": 2,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Phase differs except across $n\\lambda$ (integer $n$).\nBalls one and seven, $\\lambda/2$ apart, are in antiphase: one moves left as the other moves right, and vice versa (Figure 70)."
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "blockIndex": 3,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "In one period, a wave travels a distance $\\lambda$ (see Figure 70e).\nIts speed is therefore given by\n$v=\\frac{\\lambda}{T}$ (4.1)"
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "blockIndex": 3,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Since the period $T$ and frequency $\\nu$ are related by\n$T=\\frac{1}{\\nu}$,\nit follows that:\n$v=\\lambda\\nu$ (4.2)"
    },
    {
      "index": 7,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**Wave speed equals wavelength multiplied by the vibration frequency**."
    },
    {
      "index": 8,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "When a wave travels along a string, we can observe two kinds of periodicity."
    },
    {
      "index": 9,
      "blockIndex": 6,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Each point on the string repeats its motion periodically.\nFor harmonic sine or cosine motion, all points have the same frequency and amplitude, but differ in phase."
    },
    {
      "index": 10,
      "blockIndex": 7,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "At any instant, the profile repeats at intervals of $\\lambda$.\nFigure 72 shows this profile as the curve through $O$.\nThe whole shape moves rightward at speed $v$ as time passes."
    },
    {
      "index": 11,
      "blockIndex": 10,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "After a time interval\n$\\Delta t=\\frac{\\Delta s}{v}$\nthe wave has the shape shown by the curve that does not pass through $O$."
    },
    {
      "index": 12,
      "blockIndex": 11,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Equation (4.2), which relates wave speed, wavelength, and vibration frequency, also applies to longitudinal waves."
    }
